Do an internet search and describe the different file types for images used in games and 
why a specific type is used over another.  (Hint: bitmap is not the most preferred file format)

    Working with different image file types used in video games for source art, UI elements, and 
runtime textures is highly dependent on compression needs, transparency, and hardware performance. 
    For source and lossless formats .png, .tga, and .psd/ .tif are recommended. PNG is mostly used 
for 2D sprites, UI icons, and user interfaces as it supports enhanced details. TGA is older but 
preferred for raw 3D game textures and heightmaps as it handles uncompressed pixel data. PSD/ TIFF 
are used by artists in design software before exporting into game-ready compressed formats.
    For runtime and compressed formats .webp, .dds, and .jpeg are recommended. WebP is more modern and 
supports lossless compression with transparency; beneficial for smaller file sizes than PNG for web 
and small games. DDS/ DXTC files store GPUU-ready compressed textures that stay compressed in video 
memory for faster rendering. JPEG is a compressed format used for skyboxes or non-transparent 
background photos where file size matters more than fine pixel precision, avoided for sprites.
    For vector and special formats SVG is recommended as it uses math equations rather than pixels, 
making them ideal for resolution-independent user interference layouts.